WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:06.669
So in A Song of Ice and Fire and Game of Thrones,
Westeros is divided by a big icy Wall. To

00:00:06.669 --> 00:00:10.410
its south are the Seven Kingdoms, with all
its cities and castles and ladies and lords

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.790
and almost all the main characters in the
series. North of the Wall is a vast frozen

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:19.660
wilderness , populated by people known as
the wildlings. The wildlings are in trouble,

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.769
because recently, they’ve been under attack
from the mysterious Others, or white walkers.

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:28.470
Mance Rayder, the “King-beyond-the-Wall”
who unites and leads the wildlings, tries

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.210
to fight the white walkers at first, but
he can’t stand against them, so instead

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.510
he decides to lead his people to safety south
of the Wall. But the Night’s Watch who

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.359
man the Wall see the wildlings as enemies,
as dangerous savages, and they don’t want

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:45.500
to let them through. So Mance, determined
to get his people south, prepares to do something

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.489
drastic. He searches for an ancient artefact
said to have the “power” to “break”

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:55.140
the Wall – something called Joramun’s
Horn, or the Horn of Winter.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.539
Mance searches for the Horn in the Frostfangs, and “all over the valley of the Milkwater”,

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:04.839
digging up “half a hundred graves” of
ancient “kings and heroes”. Ygritte tells

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:09.270
Jon that for all their searching, the wildlings
never find the Horn. But later in Storm,

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:13.960
Mance says that Ygritte lied, and that he
did find it – he shows Jon a “great warhorn”,

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:19.220
eight feet long, banded in gold, and engraved
with runes. Mance says that unless the Watch

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.560
lets the wildlings through the Wall, he’ll
blow the horn and destroy the Wall, even

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.799
though he knows that if the Wall falls, there’ll
be nothing to stop the Others. In the end

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:33.450
Stannis captures Mance and Melisandre burns
the horn, and Jon lets many of the wildlings

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:38.149
through the Wall anyway. So it looks like
the story of the Horn is cut short. But later,

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:43.140
Tormund tells Jon that Mance’s horn wasn’t
actually the real Horn, that it was as an

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:48.700
empty threat. So Jon asks, “If Mance’s
horn was just a feint, where is the true horn?”

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:54.090
We might also ask what the Horn actually does,
exactly, and who made it, and why. We’re

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:58.460
never directly given answers to these questions
in the text but we can make some pretty good

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.340
guesses. Let’s start at the beginning – with
Joramun.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.479
According to legend, Joramun was “The first
King-Beyond-the-Wall”, the first person

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:10.440
to unite and lead the wildlings. He lived
thousands of years ago , so we don’t really

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.910
know anything about him for certain, but we
hear a few different stories. Jeor Mormont

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:19.550
implies that Joramun tried to invade the south,
but was defeated, either by the Wall, or by

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:24.230
the Starks of Winterfell. In another story,
Joramun apparently fought alongside the Starks

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:27.840
in the overthrow of the Night’s King , a
Lord Commander of the Night’s Watch who

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.750
seemed to be involved with the Others. But
the main thing we’re told about Joramun

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:36.690
is that he “blew the Horn of Winter, and
woke giants from the earth”, which apparently

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:41.010
means the Horn has the power to “bring down
the Wall”. So these legends raise a lot

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:45.480
of questions – like, if blowing the Horn
of Winter brings down the Wall, and Joramun

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.970
blew the Horn of Winter, why’s the Wall
still standing? Was the Horn not used to its

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:54.409
full power, or maybe not directed at the Wall
or something? And did Joramun try to invade

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:59.280
the south, or did he fight with the Starks,
against the Night’s King, or both? Was the

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.640
Horn used in these conflicts? And most of
all – what does it mean to “wake giants

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.849
from the earth”?
Fans have a lot of different ideas about what

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:11.569
this phrase means. Like, maybe it’s literal
– maybe there are giants in some kinda hibernation

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:14.840
under the ground and the sound of the horn
will wake them and make them tear down the

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:20.030
Wall. Some suggest there are ice dragons frozen
within the Wall, or huge weirwood trees that

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:24.049
could be described as giants. But there’s
evidence for a different explanation in The

00:03:24.049 --> 00:03:29.010
World of Ice and Fire, in the bit about the
Breaking of the Arm of Dorne. Basically, thousands

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:33.860
of years ago, the first humans came to Westeros
across a land bridge called the Arm of Dorne.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.180
They started warring with the children of
the forest, so to try to stop more humans

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.959
coming, the children used the magic of the
old gods to break the Arm – “the old gods

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:46.879
stirred, and giants awoke in the earth, and
all of Westeros shook and trembled. Great

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:50.670
cracks appeared in the earth, and hills and
mountains collapsed and were swallowed up.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:54.569
And then the seas came rushing in, and the
Arm of Dorne was broken”, leaving “only

00:03:54.569 --> 00:04:00.400
[the] few bare rocky islands” we see today. So George R. R. Martin uses waking giants

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.989
from the earth as a metaphor for a huge earth-shaking
cataclysm – presumably, that’s what the

00:04:05.989 --> 00:04:09.409
Horn of Winter does, and that’s how it could
destroy the Wall.

00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:13.519
This may also hint at the Horn’s origins
– it sounds like the Horn uses the magic

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:17.590
of the children of the forest. But the physical
form of a horn itself seems like something

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.530
humans would make, especially if it’s banded
in metal, which we know the children didn’t

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:26.320
use. So maybe the Horn was made by the children
of the forest and the First Men working together

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.990
– which makes sense because the Horn dates
back to a time of friendship and peace between

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:33.710
the children and the Men. In fact, it was
not long before Joramun that the children

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:37.920
and the Men apparently worked together to
defeat the Others and build the Wall – which

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.380
raises another question – why would the
children and the Men make a Horn that can

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:45.940
destroy the Wall just after they built the
Wall? Maybe this supports the theory that

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:50.030
the Wall was actually built by the Others.
But if the children and Men actually wanted

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:54.830
to destroy the Wall, why put that power into
a horn, instead of just using that power and

00:04:54.830 --> 00:05:00.040
destroying the Wall right away? Maybe the
Horn was designed not to destroy the Wall,

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:04.320
but as a power play to show that you could
bring down the Wall – kinda like nukes in

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.900
the Cold War. Being able to threaten someone
with complete destruction can be more powerful

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:13.590
than actually destroying them. This is what
Mance Rayder does in Storm, right? He doesn’t

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.430
want to use the Horn to bring down the Wall,
because he knows that would make everyone

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:20.910
vulnerable to the Others. But he threatens
to do it anyway to force the Watch to do what

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.310
he wants. Maybe that’s why the Horn was
made in the first place – not so much as

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.910
a way to destroy the Wall, but as a way to
project power by showing you could destroy

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.370
the Wall – it might have first been used
against the Night’s King. The Night’s

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.760
King apparently took over the Night’s Watch
and the Wall “with strange sorceries”

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.370
and made sacrifices to the Others . Maybe
the way the wildlings and Starks defeated

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:45.500
him was by threatening to use the Horn – maybe
Joramun sounded it at less than its full power,

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:50.410
enough to show he could destroy the Wall without
actually doing it, in doing so, forcing the

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:55.590
Night’s King to stand down. That’s just
a guess, there’s no solid evidence to support

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:59.180
this, but it does make sense for the Horn
to be designed more as a threat than to actually

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:03.300
be used.
To speculate a little more we also guess at

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:07.270
where the Horn was kept after the business
with Joramun and the Night’s King. Maybe

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:11.910
the Horn of Winter was held by the Stark “Kings
of Winter” at Winterfell. Maybe that’s

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.300
why it’s said that “there must always
be a Stark in Winterfell” – the Starks

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.870
historically guarded the Horn. Maybe the Horn
was kept in the Winterfell crypts – there

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:23.450
are lots of hints that there’s something
important down there – both Bran and Jon

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:27.990
have many dreams about the crypts. Also,
Mance Rayder has shown a suspicious interest

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.960
in the crypts – he actually infiltrates
Winterfell, twice – in the first book , and

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:37.050
in Dance – Mance is still alive in the books.
In Dance, Mance gets into Winterfell and tries

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:41.210
to find out from Theon where the crypts are. Is it possible that Mance believes that

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:45.870
the Horn of Winter is in the Winterfell crypts?
Is that why he keeps sneaking into Winterfell?

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:49.320
Maybe we’ll find out in the next book.
But it’s also possible that the Horn’s

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.210
not in Winterfell anymore, if it ever was,
because it seems likely that the Horn of Winter

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:58.630
is now in the possession of Samwell Tarly.
In Clash, Jon finds a cache of dragonglass

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:02.430
weapons wrapped in a Night’s Watch cloak
and buried on the Fist of the First Men, possibly

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:07.210
by Benjen or Coldhands for Bloodraven. Along
with the dragonglass daggers and arrowheads

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:11.960
is an “old warhorn, made from an auroch’s
horn and banded in bronze”. It’s cracked

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:16.100
and chipped and Jon can’t get any sound
from it, so he gives it to Sam, who “like[s]

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:20.810
old things, even worthless old things”.
Basically this horn doesn’t seem to be important,

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:25.220
but Sam hangs onto it and the books keep subtly
reminding us that he still has it – four

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:29.930
times in two books, the horn is offhandedly
mentioned, often right after Jon chapters

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:34.660
where he mentions the Horn of Winter. Why
would this horn keep coming up unless it was

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:39.300
important? It looks like George Martin is
setting us up to reveal that Sam’s “worthless”

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:43.470
old horn is actually the legendary Horn of
Winter.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.810
Which could lead to some pretty crazy shit
happening – like, what would happen if Sam

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:51.520
repairs and blows the Horn while he’s in
Oldtown? Would the Wall suddenly fall, hundreds

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:57.700
of miles away? Or if the Horn, is, like, directional,
would Oldtown collapse around Sam? It’s

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:01.970
also worth considering that magic in this
story, especially the magic of the old gods,

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:07.970
often has a cost of blood or human life. When
someone uses another magic horn in the story,

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:13.280
Dragonbinder, his lips blister and his chest
bleeds and he ends up dead. Whoever uses

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.180
Joramun’s Horn might suffer something similar.
There is an interesting symmetry between the

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:23.500
fiery Dragonbinder and the icy Horn of Winter…
Also there’s apparently “a magic horn”

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:27.940
that summons “krakens from the deep” which
sounds pretty sick. But anyway.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:31.160
We could speculate all day about Joramun’s
Horn, but here’re our best guesses based

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:35.330
on what we know now. According to legend,
it’s an ancient horn with the power to bring

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:38.870
down the Wall. It was probably originally
made by the children of the forest and the

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:42.370
First Men, maybe not because they wanted to
destroy the Wall, but because they wanted

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:47.520
to be able to threaten to destroy it, like
a mutally assured destruction type deal. After

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.310
Joramun and the Night’s King, the Horn may
have been kept by the Starks in the Winterfell

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:56.400
crypts, though now it seems likely that the
horn is held by Samwell Tarly in the Citadel,

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.970
which could have hilarious and/or disastrous
consequences. We’ll hopefully learn much

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:05.830
more about the Horn in the next book, it’s
apparently on the cover, after all. Until

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:10.150
then, we’ll keep on guessing.
Thanks for watching. For more Game of Thrones

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:13.810
discussion and analysis, you might like to
check out the Song of Ice and Fire subreddit

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:19.630
and Westeros.org. Many thanks to all Patrons,
